=== MUTATION CHECK — 15 mutations, one per production behaviour ===
M1  relink: drop the per_line stage                       -> 1 failed
M2  relink: drop the winners stage                        -> 14 failed
M3  relink: drop the tx_rank = line_rank diagonal         -> 2 failed
M4  relink: drop the NOT EXISTS anti-join                 -> 3 failed
M5  relink: drop bt_used.company_id                       -> 1 failed
M6  relink: drop je.company_id / bt_src.company_id        -> 1 failed
M7  relink: drop source_allocs ORDER BY                   -> 1 failed
M8  relink: stop writing transfer_journal_entry_id        -> 1 failed
M9  runner: single pass, no repeat                        -> 3 failed
M10 runner: drop the savepoint                            -> 2 failed
M11 import: LEFT JOIN gl_journal_lines instead of JOIN    -> 3 failed
M12 import: drop the per_line stage                       -> 1 failed
M13 import: drop the NOT EXISTS anti-join                 -> 2 failed
M14 import: drop bt_used.company_id                       -> 1 failed
M15 import: drop et.company_id / jl.company_id            -> 1 failed
M16 import: link one row per pass (WHERE tx_rank = 1)     -> 1 failed
M17 relink: link one row per pass (WHERE tx_rank = 1)     -> 3 failed
RESTORED                                                  -> 19 passed

M1 and M12 first SURVIVED. The mutation script had replaced the first textual
match, which is the import pass in both cases, so neither had mutated what it
named; and once aimed correctly, no test covered the case. The rank diagonal is
not by itself a matching: probed on the fixture DB, two sources four days apart
plus a row two days from each gives tx_rank = line_rank for THREE pairs, two of
them on one line.

  tx | line | date_distance | tx_rank | line_rank | on_diagonal
  d1 | f1   |             0 |       1 |         1 | t
  d2 | f2   |             0 |       1 |         1 | t
  d3 | f1   |             2 |       1 |         2 | f
  d3 | f2   |             2 |       2 |         2 | t

Without per_line the UPDATE binds 3 rows onto 2 lines. That fixture is now a
test in each file, and both mutations fail with "expected 3 to be 2".

M7 is deterministic only because its fixture inserts the higher-id journal leg
FIRST, so heap order and id order disagree.

M16/M17 guard the round-3 claim that the rank pairing settles N contenders in ONE
statement. `TransferLinkResult.passes` reports the linking statements a call ran,
so both single-statement tests assert passes === 1 rather than only the row count,
which one-row-per-pass would satisfy just as well.

NOT mutation-proved: the two import routes now pass `result.errors` out as
`warnings` (bank-transactions-bulk-commit, bank-feeds/[id]/extension-ingest).
That is a one-line mapping in each, and neither route has a unit-test harness in
this repo, so nothing fails if it is removed. The service half — the import
result carrying the entry when a pass reports failed — is covered by two unit
tests in bankImportService.bulk.test.ts.

=== FULL UNIT SUITE (bunx vitest run, DATABASE_URL and fixture env UNSET) ===
With the default 10s test timeout this suite is flaky on this box, and this
branch trips more of it than origin/master does. Interleaved A/B, same tree,
alternating runs:

  BASE 1 (load 163)   6 failed | 9314 passed
  MINE 1 (load 176)  11 failed | 9311 passed
  BASE 2 (load 147)   1 failed | 9319 passed
  MINE 2 (load 141)  10 failed | 9312 passed

Every one of those failures is `Test timed out in 10000ms` or an assertion
cascading inside a file after one of its tests timed out. The same suite with
the timeout raised is completely green:

  bunx vitest run --testTimeout=60000
  Test Files  784 passed | 36 skipped (820)
       Tests  9322 passed | 234 skipped (9556)

So no assertion fails on this branch. What is true, and worth saying plainly, is
that the branch is slower to the point of tripping more borderline timeouts when
the machine is at load 140-175 (a dozen parallel agent worktrees running their
own suites). The failing files — trackerExclusion, bankAllocationService,
bankAllocationDateShift, bankAllocationUnwind, cashFlowReport — all pass in
isolation and none of them imports bankTransferLinkService,
bankTransferLinkPasses, bankImportService or the relink route, so there is no
module path from this diff to them. Reverting the UI page alone did not change
the count, and neither did removing the two new unit tests.

